swehouse.c: swehouse-analytic-cusp-speeds.patch, rebased onto a tree with
the ten small patches already applied

Not a new fix. This is the same content as swehouse-analytic-cusp-speeds.patch,
regenerated against your tree instead of a pristine one, because that patch
cannot apply where it currently stands.

The reason is in the cover letter, easy to miss among eleven patches: the
analytic patch contains six of the ten small ones outright - makransky,
krusinski, porphyry, whole-sign, koch and radians - because they touch the
same functions its derivatives replace. Your quilt stack has all ten applied,
which means six regions the analytic patch expects to find untouched are
already rewritten, in the same direction but not the same diff, so patch
areas overlap exactly as you saw. The other four small ones (apc-equator,
gauquelin, sidereal, sunshine) are independent of the analytic patch and of
each other, and are already sitting cleanly in your tree - nothing here
touches them again.

How this was built: two fresh copies of the pristine v2.10.3bfinal
swehouse.c. One took your ten patches, in the same order quilt applied them.
The other took the four independent patches plus the original analytic
patch - the "take everything" combination the cover letter's short version
names (1, 2, 3, 10 and 11 there). Diffing those two files against each other
is this patch; applying it to a copy of the first file reproduces the second
one byte for byte, which is what was checked rather than assumed. Nothing
here was hand-edited.

Verified beyond the round trip: compiled standalone against
external/swisseph's own headers, MSVC 19.51, /O2 /fp:precise /W3, zero
warnings; the resulting object disassembles to 875 multiply instructions and
zero vfmadd/vfmsub/vfnmadd/vfnmsub - no FMA contraction, same check this
project runs on every C artifact it ships.

How to test: apply this on top of your current tree (the one with 01
through 10 already in) and run the same commands the analytic patch's own
header gives, starting with the thread's own case:

  swetest -b1.1.2000 -ut12:00 -p -house-10.46061837,66.5607089,Y -speed -eswe

Cusp 1 and cusp 7 should both read 2890205.341589 degrees a day, matching
the Ascendant printed two lines below - the identity the whole thread turned
on. Everything else documented for patch 11 - the singular-point table, the
Richardson-extrapolated error figures, the four unfixed limitations, the
cost table - is unchanged; this patch does not alter what the analytic patch
does, only what tree it lands on.
